<!--
  Why this says true, and where the real rule lives.

  With no network security config at all, a targetSdk 28 or higher app is
  refused plain http to everything but loopback. Both LAN presets point at an
  address on the user's own network - the configuration that sends nothing off
  it - so without this file the option this ROM's users most want cannot
  connect, and "The brain could not be reached." is all they are told.
  android:usesCleartextTraffic is not the answer either: on Android 17 the
  attribute is inert, so declaring it would suggest a protection that is not
  there.

  What this app wants to permit is "any private literal": loopback, 10/8,
  172.16/12, 192.168/16, 100.64/10, 169.254/16 and fc00::/7. A network security
  config cannot say that - a domain entry is a name matched by suffix, and
  there is no CIDR form - so the base config permits cleartext and the address
  rule stays in code, where it is checked twice before a byte leaves:
  BrainUrl.reject refuses a plain-http base URL whose host is not a private
  literal, and OpenAiCompatClient.send parses the endpoint with java.net.URL
  and refuses again. A name is never private, however it is spelled.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <!-- System CAs only. A user-installed CA has no business on the
                 path between this app and the endpoint the user typed. -->
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
